## 8. The supplier map

Multiple patterning links several equipment categories.

ASML supplies the lithography scanners. Applied Materials and Lam Research participate in deposition and etch steps that become especially important in self-aligned schemes. KLA and other process-control vendors help measure overlay, dimensions and defects created across the repeated flow.

Mask suppliers and resist/material suppliers also see more work when the process uses more masks and exposures.

This is why one architectural choice in patterning can change demand across several parts of wafer-fab equipment.

## 9. The analyst's mental model

When someone says a node has a tighter pitch, ask:

1. Can that pitch be printed in one exposure?
2. If not, how many patterning passes are required?
3. Which steps are lithography versus deposition/etch-driven?
4. How much extra overlay control does the scheme require?
5. What happens to wafer cycle time and tool demand?
6. Could EUV or a new architecture remove some of those passes?

That translation matters because two nodes with similar headline dimensions can have very different manufacturing complexity and cost.

## Remember these 5 things

1. Multiple patterning splits one dense logical layer into several easier physical patterning steps.

2. It extends resolution, but increases process count, cost, cycle time and often overlay burden.

3. Self-aligned schemes use deposited spacers to create denser patterns, shifting work toward deposition and etch.

4. EUV can be valuable because one EUV exposure may replace several DUV-centered patterning steps on some layers.

5. The useful economic unit is the full patterning flow required to create a good feature, not the sticker price of one scanner.

## Quick check

Why can replacing several DUV patterning passes with one EUV pass be economically attractive even if the EUV scanner itself is much more expensive?

Because the EUV pass may eliminate additional masks, exposures, deposition/etch steps, metrology, queue time and overlay risk. The comparison must be made at the full process-flow level.

